Z||000007||Phantom Rage||04-22-2001||07:38 AM||||Gixxer I’m sure you are correct, please don’t feel like I corrected you.  It sounds like you really know your fuel injection.  I’m asking because I don’t.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Baldlobo I guess I’m asking the wrong question.  I am having a hard time putting it into words.  Let me try this again, I know I was not very clear the first time around.  Assume that you have a built up motor utilizing all the safety requirements for a turbo application, except for that of the fuel injection.  Would an increase of one psi of gas for every one pound of boost be sufficient to keep a motor from detonating, given that everything else has also been tuned correctly?  I hope this is a better question.  <P>What this is all stemming from is that I have found, from a very reputable company, a boost compensating fuel pressure regulator for cheap.  This company is stating as previous mentioned a one pound increase of fuel pressure to one psi of boost.  Please correct me if I am wrong, but isn’t that a 1:1 ratio?  Car-Tech as well as Vortech state anywhere from a 2:1 to 12:1for their bcfpr.  Does it seem like 1:1 is a little anemic?<BR>I’m starting to think that it was only designed for factory equipped turbo vehicles, which have minor mods.            <P>From my current understanding, there is an “in general” limit to boost compensating fuel pressure regulators usually somewhere in the neighborhood of 8-10 pounds of boost, E.g. (Car-Tech, Vortech.)  I assume that this would also hold true for this unit as well.<P>I guess I should probably just give them a call.<P>Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||7
Z||000008||Phantom Rage||04-22-2001||07:38 AM||||Gixxer I’m sure you are correct, please don’t feel like I corrected you.  It sounds like you really know your fuel injection.  I’m asking because I don’t.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Baldlobo I guess I’m asking the wrong question.  I am having a hard time putting it into words.  Let me try this again, I know I was not very clear the first time around.  Assume that you have a built up motor utilizing all the safety requirements for a turbo application, except for that of the fuel injection.  Would an increase of one psi of gas for every one pound of boost be sufficient to keep a motor from detonating, given that everything else has also been tuned correctly?  I hope this is a better question.  <P>What this is all stemming from is that I have found, from a very reputable company, a boost compensating fuel pressure regulator for cheap.  This company is stating as previous mentioned a one pound increase of fuel pressure to one psi of boost.  Please correct me if I am wrong, but isn’t that a 1:1 ratio?  Car-Tech as well as Vortech state anywhere from a 2:1 to 12:1for their bcfpr.  Does it seem like 1:1 is a little anemic?<BR>I’m starting to think that it was only designed for factory equipped turbo vehicles, which have minor mods.            <P>From my current understanding, there is an “in general” limit to boost compensating fuel pressure regulators usually somewhere in the neighborhood of 8-10 pounds of boost, E.g. (Car-Tech, Vortech.)  I assume that this would also hold true for this unit as well.<P>I guess I should probably just give them a call.<P>Thanks  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||7
Z||000009||hugh||04-22-2001||08:09 AM||||For fuel injectors to work properly it is necessary to have a pressure differential between the nozzle and the intake manifold for proper atomization of the fuel.If that differential is 35 lbs.,as in my Grand National the regulator will raise the fuel pressure to keep the same difference as the boost goes up.Here is how I set mine.<BR>   1st,I remove the manifold sensing line ,plug it and leave the regulator sense only atmospheric pressure.<BR>   2nd,I set the fuel pressure at atmospheric pressure (which is all the regulator sees with the manifold line removed)and the engine idling.<BR>   3rd,I then reattach the manifold line,at this point the fuel pressure will drop because the manifold at an idle is in a vacuum,but will still hold that 35lbs.difference.As boost goes up the fuel pressure will also go up to hold that difference.The regulator controls pressure to the injectors by varying the fuel return to the tank(more fuel returned to the tank lower pressure to injectors,less fuel returned,more pressure)<BR>I should have mentioned I have an adjustable regulator on my engine which is not stock.The stock regulator is nonadjustable,though some people have slightly crushed the top with a hammer and socket to raise fuel pressure(NOT a good idea)<p>[This message has been edited by hugh (edited 04-22-2001).]||||reg||1
